    BEGIN {
      package Foo; use Test::Builder; require Exporter;
      our @ISA = ('Exporter'); our @EXPORT = ('ok');
      my $TB = Test::Builder->new;
      sub ok { my ($ok, $msg) = @_; our $d; die "deep\n" if ++$d > 5; $TB->ok($ok, $msg); return $ok }
      $INC{'Foo.pm'} = 1;
    }
    use Foo;
    Test::Builder->new->plan(tests => 2);
    ok(1, "one"); ok(1, "two");
perl: 1..2 / ok 1 - one / ok 2 - two.  PCL (main 5faf9c8f): 1..2 then "deep" (without the guard: SBCL "Binding stack exhausted", the frames alternating (LAMBDA ...) / P-METHOD-CALL with arguments (1, "running test_expect()") -- exactly #3869's backtrace).  Same with a glob install `*main::ok = \&Foo::ok`.

CAUSE: every CODE install into a package -- p-import-perl-symbol (Exporter), %p-glob-assign-slots' two code arms, %p-glob-copy-code-slot -- did `(setf (fdefinition (intern NAME pkg)) fn)`.  A user package :uses :pcl, so `(intern "PL-OK" main)` is the INHERITED pcl::pl-ok, the TAP layer's ok: the install redefined it for the whole program.  Test::Builder's ->ok method (cl/pcl-test.lisp) calls pcl's pl-ok -- now Template::Test::ok, which calls $TB->ok again.

FIX (one mechanism, cl/pcl-runtime.lisp): %p-own-code-symbol PKG NAME INCOMING -- an INHERITED name is SHADOWED first (through %p-symref-intern, which drops the symbol memo), unless INCOMING is the very function it already holds (Test::More's own exports stay one shared symbol).  The four install sites call it.  perl's `*main::ok = \&Foo::ok` changes main's stash only.  The reader then reads main's later `ok(...)` as main's own symbol (the shadow happens at the `use`, before the next top-level form is read -- the #3405 section-head reasoning).
RESIDUE (not fixed): a call READ before a RUN-TIME install (a sub body above a run-time `*main::ok = ...`) keeps calling the runtime's ok -- the reader already chose the symbol; rare.
